TrebolToken_2.report:
**Lack of Re-Entrancy Protection** *(High)*:  
   **Description:**  
   The `transfer` and `transferFrom` functions modify the state variables `balances` before emitting the `Transfer` event. If an external contract is called as part of the fallback function when receiving tokens, it can exploit this by re-entering the contract and manipulating the balances. This is a classic re-entrancy vulnerability.  

   **Impact:**  
   Attackers can drain tokens by repeatedly calling the contract and manipulating the balances before the state is updated.  

   **Mitigation:**  
   ```solidity
   // Add a re-entrancy guard
   bool private locked;

   modifier noReentrancy() {
       require(!locked, "Reentrant call");
       locked = true;
       _;
       locked = false;
   }

   function transfer(address to, uint value) public noReentrancy returns(bool) {
       require(balanceOf(msg.sender) >= value, 'balance too low');
       balances[to] += value;
       balances[msg.sender] -= value;
       emit Transfer(msg.sender, to, value);
       return true;
   }

   function transferFrom(address from, address to, uint value) public noReentrancy returns(bool) {
       require(balanceOf(from) >= value, 'balance too low');
       require(allowance[from][msg.sender] >= value, 'allowance too low');
       balances[to] += value;
       balances[from] -= value;
       emit Transfer(from, to, value);
       return true;
   }
   ```

